From de1d41abe72d5e8e017c8d8fb8804a4e9361bcaf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 01:26:48 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 09291/11362] Seed an OKF knowledge bundle with the claims that
 were withdrawn tonight

Retractions are the seed content on purpose. They are the hardest thing to
reconstruct later and the most expensive to get wrong, because the natural
failure is that someone re-proposes a refuted lever. Five claims were withdrawn
on 2026-08-17 and each survived only in a commit body, discoverable by someone
who already knew to look.

Open Knowledge Format v0.2: plain .md, YAML frontmatter, git-native, no central
authority and no required tooling. Chosen because three of its optional field
families already match disciplines this project enforces by hand -- sources for
provenance, generated/verified for the fabrication gate, and status/stale_after
for never-prune and the freshness lint. status: deprecated with a superseded_by
link is exactly the shape a retraction wants, and it keeps the record rather
than deleting it.

The five: the marginal-band null (every confirmation shared one broken nudge);
the cross-level carry null (the lever never fired, so the A/B measured nothing);
the +0.054 delta (true on its own cell, too narrow to describe the lever --
superseded, not retracted); the seed-anchoring mechanism (refuted 6/6 paired,
and its counterexample was in the table that proposed it); and budget pooling
(impossible -- action_counter is per-instance, there is no pool).

Three standing claims are included so the retractions point somewhere: the
goal predicate never firing on a real win, seeded repair's conversion rate, and
that carried engines do not transfer. Both scored submissions are recorded, and
the 2026-06-30 entry states plainly that its configuration is NOT recoverable --
that gap is the argument for the config fingerprint.

Known gaps are written into the index rather than left implied: OKF links are
untyped so superseded_by is a local convention; the spec is deliberately
permissive about broken links where we would want a lint that fails; n=2 on
scored submissions cannot support attribution; and nothing yet checks that a
claim's numbers match the artifacts it cites.

11 documents, all conformant, no dangling internal links.

